{
  "dom_id": "step-q18697",
  "name": "Question 1",
  "problem": "Let $(x,y)$ be the solution of the following system. What is the value of $x-y?$\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}0.04x-0.08y-4=0 \\\\ 2x-3y=4\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "worked_solution": "To get rid of the decimals, we multiply the top equation by $100,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}0.04x-0.08y-4=0 & |\\,\\times 100 \\\\ 2x-3y=4 & \\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}4x-8y-400=0 \\\\ 2x-3y=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that if we multiply the second equation by $-2,$ we will get a $-4x$ term in the second equation that can cancel with the $4x$ term in the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}4x-8y-400=0 &  \\\\ 2x-3y=4 & |\\,\\times (-2)\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}4x-8y-400=0 \\\\ -4x+6y=-8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations eliminates the $x$-variable and allows us to solve for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\cancel{4x}-8y-400 & =0 \\\\ -\\cancel{4x}+6y & =-8 \\\\ (-8y+6y)-400 & =-8 \\\\ -2y & =392 \\\\ y & =-196\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can substitute $y=-196$ into the equation $2x-3y=4$ to solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-3y & =4 \\\\ 2x-3(-196) & =4 \\\\ 2x+588 & =4 \\\\ 2x & =-584 \\\\ x & =-292\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the solution to the system of equations is $x=-292$ and $y=-196.$ So, \n\n$$\nx-y=-292-(-196)=-292+196=-96.\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
